WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.310 --> 00:00:04.320
I grew up just
absolutely loving movies.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.420
My first movie that my
dad took me to was E.T.,

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.580
and I had a popcorn
bucket, like, up to here.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.670
I could barely see over it.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:12.920
Really the thing that got
me into visual effects

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:18.060
is how it is a blend of
artistry and technology.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.860
We create an illusion
that transports people

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.000
into a different place.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.040
In 2010, we created
Atomic Fiction,

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.420
which is a visual effects
and animation studio.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.180
One of the most challenging
parts of doing visual effects

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.540
is called rendering.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.800
That process can take
anywhere between 2 minutes

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.990
and 40 hours for every
single frame, every 24th

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:44.250
of a second of a film.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.810
Rendering takes an immense
amount of computing horsepower.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:51.020
Using Google Cloud Platform
to do our rendering,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.470
we have massive infrastructure
offsite when we need it

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:56.630
and then nothing when we don't.

00:00:56.630 --> 00:01:00.340
It allows us to use computers
for minutes at a time

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:02.290
and only get billed
for what we use.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.080
And so we took that
as an opportunity

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.070
to create our own tool set
more specific to our industry

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:10.980
called Conductor, which runs on
top of Google's Cloud Platform.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.310
It helped us put the resources
on where it was important--

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.000
the artists-- and making
sure that every penny

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.190
that our clients spent
made it up on the screen.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.850
These movies that might have
otherwise been bottled up

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.920
in a filmmaker's mind are
now actually seeing the light

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.050
of day on the movie screen.

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:32.370
There's no greater satisfaction
than creating a visual effect

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:36.080
that people just believe
that they're somewhere else.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.230
That's what we do is
we create fiction.

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:40.590
Hopefully it's just the
most believable fiction

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.740
that you can imagine.

